  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1124 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nRinse and repeat as upper-level ridging, currently centered over\nthe Ohio River Valley, continues to drive weather across the Mid-\nSouth. Another chilly evening is present under clear skies with\nmostly calm winds at the surface. One change worth noting\nThursday is a slight surge in dewpoints as increased Gulf\nmoisture ushers into our forecast area. PWATs will also begin to\nsurge slightly Thursday from around 0.3\" to around 0.9\". Fire\nweather concerns will be decreased moving into the weekend thanks\nto this slight surge in moisture. Otherwise, our warming trend\nwill continue through Saturday as upper-level southwest flow\nbegins to build in across the Mississippi Valley.\n\nAhead of our next system Sunday, cloud cover will begin to\nincrease Saturday with PWATs also beginning to surge further, to\naround 1.2\". Ensembles continue to hint at our next system\nbringing our next shot at rainfall as an upper-level low begins\nto rotate into the Upper Great Lakes region. An attendant cold\nfront will begin pushing across the Mid-South Sunday with\nforecast PWATs surging further to around 1.4\", just below the\n90th percentile for this time of the year. The latest run of the\nGEFS and ENS is indicating around a 20-30% chance of 500 J/kg of\nMUCAPE creeping into north Mississippi Sunday hinting at very\nisolated thunder chances. However, this slight surge in\ninstability looks to be displaced from the main front, where lift\nfor precipitation will lie. Therefore, appreciable rainfall is\nnot anticipated Sunday with latest LREF guidance hinting at\naround a 30-50% chance of exceeding a quarter of an inch of total\nrainfall through Monday morning.\n\nPerhaps the more notable influence from Sunday's front will be\npost-frontal temperatures as cooler, drier air begins to filter\nin behind this front. Early workweek, temperatures will span the\n50s to lower 60s with lows falling back into the 30s and 40s.\nLatest NBM probs have a 30-50% chance of morning lows dropping\nbelow freezing in areas mainly over West Tennessee Tuesday\nmorning. Cooler temperatures will remain the trend through next\nweek as a cool, dry airmass remains in place. Drought conditions\nwill continue to worsen over the next seven days as by Monday\nmorning, precipitation chances will quickly wane.\n\nAEH",
